00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.360
So let's talk a bit
about defense in depth.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:45.007
Why now?

00:01:45.007 --> 00:01:46.340
Why do we need defense in depth?

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:52.560
Why is this such a burning
topic at this point in time?

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.250
So one of the reasons
why defense in depth

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.260
is becoming more
important is because

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.710
of some of the emerging
trends in application,

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:04.520
both stack as well
as infrastructure.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.280
Some of the trends that we see
is the adoption of containers

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.770
and microservices.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:12.020
We see a rapid growth in
adoption of both paradigms.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:17.390
Further, we see that the
deployment of these systems

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:20.060
happen to be on public
cloud, sometimes

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.750
on multiple clouds, and hybrid.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.910
Lastly, the adoption
of microservices

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.010
also means that
every service can be

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.730
built in a separate language.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:34.200
So we see a lot of heterogeneity
in the application stacks,

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.232
as well as in how they're
packaged and deployed.

00:02:36.232 --> 00:02:38.690
There may be something that
you might call a service that's

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:42.325
deployed on a VM somewhere,
because it is a legacy system,

00:02:42.325 --> 00:02:43.950
and then you build
your next generation

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.140
little microservices,
as you might call it,

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.360
and that's deployed as a
container in a Kubernetes

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:49.220
environment.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.740
So we see heterogeneity
across the board,

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.670
and we all just see
a lot of services

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:55.670
that talk to each other.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.086
So as you can see, there's a
bit of a communication sprawl

00:02:58.086 --> 00:03:01.790
that happens within
your network, which

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.400
is far bigger than what used
to be the case, when you had

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.280
a clean three-tier
web app-based solution

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.100
or if you had a legacy system.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.670
So how does this
impact security?

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.520
So first of all, we saw that
there was a lot of connections

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.020
between the services.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.840
The connections also represent
a surface area of attack.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.180
So there's a much greater
surface area of attack

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.150
if your perimeter is breached.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:34.330
Further, as the
example here shows,

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:37.810
a compromise of a
shared service can

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:40.630
result in replay
attacks of identities

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.130
of a whole lot of
other services.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:45.400
So your shared service can
escalate the privilege that

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.940
is actually available to it.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:50.140
So here in this example,
we have a logging service

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.530
which you might think is
not that security-critical,

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.930
but imagine if it
was compromised.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.270
Almost all your services
are authenticating

00:03:58.270 --> 00:03:59.621
to the logging service.

00:03:59.621 --> 00:04:01.120
And what we have
seen, another trend

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.410
that we have seen of late, is
that option of arrest as a way

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:09.160
to communicate between
services, and the use of bearer

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:12.172
tokens like JWT, for example,
or some opaque OAuth token,

00:04:12.172 --> 00:04:14.130
but effectively a bearer
token, to authenticate

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:15.850
as part of the REST code.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:18.140
So one of the challenges
of bearer token

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:20.860
is that a bearer
token can be replayed

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:27.490
by the recipient or anyone else
who is listening on the wire.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.760
So thus, these credentials,
which you present as tokens,

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:33.160
can be replayed by a compromised
service like the logging

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:36.970
service, which can now suddenly
pretend to be service A

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:42.700
and start accessing data
from service B for data which

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.010
it should not have been
authorized to access.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:48.310
So thus, this
impersonation is a problem

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.040
that we see because
of the increasing

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.370
use of bearer tokens as part
of our modern deployments.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.760
The last point is,
as I mentioned,

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.990
your networks, your network
infrastructures itself.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:06.740
Anyone who has access to
listen in on the network

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.410
can effectively steal
all user credentials.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.810
Therefore your root in
network can effectively

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.520
become root in production, and
that's an undesirable security

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:15.020
property.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.400
So what are the principles
of defense in depth

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:31.300
that we aspire to build as
part of our infrastructure?

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.240
So the first is you do create
a secure network perimeter.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.880
The perimeter doesn't go away.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.280
There is a value to having
a network perimeter,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.430
and as Google, we have our
network perimeter, which

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.650
we, obviously, strongly defend.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.520
But once you have built
your network perimeter

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.950
and you have done all
the hardening of it,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.460
you effectively then
assume that your perimeter

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.020
has been breached.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.170
Right after you build it you
assume it has been breached.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.660
You assume that the threat
is inside your perimeter.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.650
So thus, you now
have every service

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.600
that needs to
effectively establish

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.240
trust with any other
service that talks to it.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.150
So in some sense, at a very
logical high level view,

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.940
every service has
its own firewall.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:18.480
So you can think of them
as micro-perimeters.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.180
And at this point
in time, because you

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.882
need to now monitor not just
the edge of your network,

00:06:23.882 --> 00:06:26.340
you need to have some central
administration and monitoring

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.100
of every service endpoint that
exists within your network.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.900
This posture has been
popularized as a term called

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.970
zero trust in the industry.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.810
The term, I believe, originated
from an analyst in Forrester,

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:44.460
but now has been
increasingly been

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.210
adopted by a number
of security vendors.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.640
Effectively, it's a way of
achieving defense in depth.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.540
The key principle
behind it is that you

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:55.320
have peer-to-peer
trust models, and you

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:59.110
don't trust your
network by default.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:02.140
Just because an
entity happens to be

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:05.000
within your network doesn't
mean it should be trusted,

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.540
even if it has a valid
application level credentials.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:10.270
And we'll talk a bit
about the difference

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:14.310
between various layers
of identities that inform

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:15.690
a security posture like this.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:24.770
So we talked about creating
a secure perimeter,

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.860
and Google allows you to
do so on a cloud basis

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:31.370
across all different
computational and service

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:32.236
types.

00:07:32.236 --> 00:07:33.735
So if you have your
traditional VMs,

00:07:33.735 --> 00:07:35.990
we have VPC networks
and firewalls.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:39.080
We recently launched
to Kubernetes engine

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:42.110
private clusters that allow
you to build the perimeter

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:45.480
security for containerized
workloads running

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:48.530
in Google Kubernetes engine.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:53.330
And one of the products that
is in private beta right now

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.160
is VPC service controls.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:56.990
And VPC service
controls allows you

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:59.390
to extend this notion
of perimeter security

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:03.990
to fully manage services like
Google Cloud Storage or bit

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.410
query data sets, for example.

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.620
Now, having said that,
this is important to do.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:13.500
One of the main reasons to
have an secure perimeter

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:14.910
is to mitigate
exfiltration risk.

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:18.540
Because you need some
chokepoint for you to control

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.360
outbound communication
to the internet.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.190
And that's one of the big value
propositions of having this.

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:27.360
But having done that, you
need to think about defense

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.370
in depth, which this
session is about.

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:32.909
So we are going to
start with two concepts

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:35.600
that we have adopted
within Google

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.789
and on which our defense
in depth posture is built.

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.309
The first is BeyondCorp,
which has perhaps

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:43.620
been a little more
popular and many of you

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.550
might have heard about it.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:49.320
And the other is application
layer transport security,

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.090
which is a paper
that we have written,

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:53.310
but it also informs some
of the key principles

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:58.070
of our production security.

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:03.060
So talking about BeyondCorp,
BeyondCorp effectively

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:07.080
is mainly applicable to a
human being, like an employee,

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:11.040
accessing sensitive data
in a corporate application,

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:13.540
for example, from their laptop.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:15.630
So one of the first things
you want to make sure

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:18.300
is that you have a
strong identity that

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:21.005
is phishing-resistant
and a phishing-resistant

00:09:21.005 --> 00:09:21.630
authentication.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:23.796
I think we've seen a few
articles float by recently,

00:09:23.796 --> 00:09:25.830
saying none of the
Google employees

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:30.000
have been phished in
the last few years.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.710
The second is having
well-manage devices, which

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.770
are a part of your
security posture,

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:41.100
and they are actually-- the
status of your device actually

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.100
informs access.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.850
Having well-configured TLS to
protect the data in transit.

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:50.650
Having a policy engine
that can actually

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.750
evaluate this
multi-factors in order

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:55.960
to decide what level of
risk that the request has

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.750
and then grant you access
based upon the risk assessment

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:00.940
of their specific request.

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:03.610
And a policy enforcement
point, which would typically

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:07.450
be reverse proxy on
your network edge

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:12.020
that can calculate all
of these attributes

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:14.920
and terminate the TLS, and
then that then forwards it

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:18.220
to the systems that are then
within your network perimeter

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:19.160
that you created.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:21.820
This is BeyondCorp,
and as I said,

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:24.730
just because your
employee has a VPN

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:28.360
and is able to join the
network, every device

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.730
that joins your network
via VPN should not

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:33.490
have equivalent privileges
because your devices may

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:36.280
have different levels of trust.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:38.110
If you were to use a
VPN software to join

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:40.152
in from a shared
desktop in a hotel,

00:10:40.152 --> 00:10:41.860
it has very different
security properties

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:44.260
than using a very
well-managed desktop

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:48.760
in your corporate network,
and again, your mobile device

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.800
may have a different
security posture.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.820
Thus they're not all equivalent.

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:57.430
And you need to have
application-level understanding

00:10:57.430 --> 00:11:01.660
of the device and the
data being accessed.

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:03.850
Just having an LT understanding
tells you, can you

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.040
access this endpoint or
not, but you may actually

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:09.040
want to answer questions
like if you're coming in

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:13.630
from a mobile device, can
you access your compensation,

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:14.620
for example?

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:16.180
Yes or no, maybe yes.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:18.190
Can you access the
compensation of your reports

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:19.898
and their reports and
so on and so forth?

00:11:19.898 --> 00:11:22.040
Maybe the answer is
no, because if you

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.610
were to be senior enough
in your organization

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:29.790
and your mobile device
was lost or compromised,

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:32.020
a broach like that
would then result

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:35.400
in the leak of a large
section of employees,

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:39.265
their compensations can
be leaked, as an example.

00:11:39.265 --> 00:11:41.890
Thus you can see it requires an
application-level understanding

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:44.470
of the posture, and it's
not just a network problem.

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:50.802
Application layer
transport security--

00:11:50.802 --> 00:11:52.260
application layer
transfer security

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:55.110
is the counterpart to
what we do with BeyondCorp

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:58.230
but is optimized for
service-to-service

00:11:58.230 --> 00:11:59.160
interactions.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:02.160
And this particular
technology that we have built

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:05.190
has been optimized for Google's
production environment.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:09.390
It is based upon the
same notions of not

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:12.990
trusting the network by default
but rather having strong peer

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:16.490
authentication and encryption in
transit, just like BeyondCorp.

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:19.990
But one of the key
principles, again,

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:21.960
just like we talked
about before,

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:25.200
is having this notion of
being able to establish

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:27.210
this trust at the
application layer

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:31.170
and remove any dependency on the
security of the network path.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:35.025
So you don't have root
in network becoming root

00:12:35.025 --> 00:12:37.380
in production very easy.

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:40.830
And lastly, the way to
implement this at scale--

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.770
and Google has hundreds
of thousands of services,

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.200
millions of endpoints--
is to be transparent

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:47.880
from the application code.

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:50.815
That's how we can enforce
this by default, using just

00:12:50.815 --> 00:12:51.315
policies.

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:55.780
So how does ALTS work?

00:12:55.780 --> 00:13:00.460
I'll go through this fairly
quickly but effectively.

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:04.630
ALTS consists of-- it's a mutual
authentication and transport

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:07.720
encryption system that
was developed by Google.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:10.960
In some sense, it is
similar in concept

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:15.280
to mTLS, or Mutually
Authenticated TLS.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:18.310
One of the key
aspects of ALTS is

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:21.940
that it authenticates the
identities of the service

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:24.520
and not the hosts.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:26.710
So instead of authenticating
the IP address,

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:31.000
it authenticates identities
that are bound to services.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:34.120
This trust model of
authenticating identities

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:37.630
makes it easy to replicate
load balance and reschedule

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:40.120
microservices across hosts.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:43.570
So it enables the agility of
your authorization policies

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:47.080
and the resilience of your
authorization policies.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:49.000
So the ALTS relies
on two protocols.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.250
There's a handshake
protocol to establish

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:55.770
a key and a record protocol
to send the protected RPC.

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:57.370
The handshake
protocol is a Diffie

00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:01.300
Hellmann-based authenticated
exchange protocol.

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:05.390
It supports perfect forward
secrecy and session resumption.

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:09.030
The record protocol
encrypts and authenticates

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:12.530
network traffic
using the protocol

00:14:12.530 --> 00:14:15.310
secret that's been established
through the handshake protocol.

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:23.270
So that's great, Google
does this, but how

00:14:23.270 --> 00:14:26.330
do you implement
something like this?

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:29.199
So one of the principles we
have within Google Cloud, one

00:14:29.199 --> 00:14:30.740
of the approaches
that we are taking,

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:33.980
is that we want to
make these concepts be

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:39.290
accessible to our users
both on Google Cloud

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:42.050
and as part of a hybrid setup.

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:45.180
And towards that, for example,
the principles of BeyondCorp,

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:48.080
we are incrementally
building it into products,

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:52.340
like the identity of a proxy,
and VPC service controls,

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:54.620
and the principles of
application layer transport

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:57.070
security have been
built into Istio.

00:14:57.070 --> 00:15:01.040
Today we'll talk more about
how we built this into Istio.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:03.680
For a moment we'll do a
very quick, brief intro

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:05.960
of what is Istio, for
those who are looking

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:07.322
at this for the first time.

00:15:07.322 --> 00:15:08.780
Given that this is
a security talk,

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:11.240
I'm going to speed
through this section.

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:14.000
Feel free to ask me
questions about it at the end

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:15.970
if you have more.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:19.020
So Istio is an open
services platform

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:22.550
that's built to manage service
interactions on container

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:24.120
and VM-based workloads.

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:27.040
So it is built to handle
this kind of heterogeneity,

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:28.670
so it's not just a
Kubernetes thing,

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:31.770
though it's highly optimized
to work with Kubernetes.

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:34.490
Istio incorporates the
principles and learnings

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:38.030
that we have learned from
securing Google's production

00:15:38.030 --> 00:15:42.340
environment with technologies
like ALTS and more.

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:47.820
While Google is
one of the founders

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:51.760
and significant
contributor to Istio,

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:56.710
there are other major
technology providers

00:15:56.710 --> 00:15:58.540
who have also
contributed to Istio,

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:02.410
most notably Lyft, who
created the envoy proxy

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.670
that we have adopted as part
of Istio's technology stack.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.810
So why should you
care about Istio?

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.990
Maybe you already have
a bunch of software

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:21.780
that does similar things that
allows you to create TLS,

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.180
allows you to observe traffic.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.520
These are not new concepts.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:28.770
The main value of
Istio, though, is

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:31.950
that it's an integrated platform
for a broad set of service

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:33.710
level network functions.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:35.850
A couple of things--
one, we elevate

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:40.080
the principles to a service
level, versus thinking of them

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:41.160
at the network level.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:43.590
And B, you get a single
integrated platform

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:46.650
that you can use both
for traffic management,

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:48.750
for observability,
like the latency

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:51.670
graphs that we showed
in our keynote earlier,

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:54.400
as well as for security.

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:57.600
Today we will focus on
policy-driven security,

00:16:57.600 --> 00:16:59.880
and Istio implements that.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:02.970
Before we dive into the
actual security pieces,

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:06.510
it's good to have a quick
overview of the components that

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.040
make up Istio.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:12.780
So Istio itself
has a control plane

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:14.470
that consists of three things.

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:15.780
The first is Pilot.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:20.700
Pilot is a control plane
to configure and push

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:23.160
service communication policies,
like, for example, service

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:28.290
discovery and even
authorization policies that

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:32.190
run within the envoy proxy.

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:33.165
The second is Mixer.

00:17:33.165 --> 00:17:41.190
Mixer provides a pluggability
interface for you

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:45.870
to integrate both destinations
for monitoring and metrics

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:50.700
as well as policy enforcement
for policy providers.

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:54.480
Citadel is a core part
of Istio security,

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:58.830
and it enables you to
configure mutual TLS seamlessly

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:04.620
across a vast set
of services, and on

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:06.480
a heterogeneous infrastructure.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:09.990
And it also provides a built-in
identity and credential

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:12.340
management.

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:15.280
So why do we use
proxies within Istio?

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:18.520
Mostly to normalize
across the heterogeneity.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.950
The proxies give us
this normalization layer

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:22.660
and an abstraction
layer for us to be

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:25.630
able to have a homogeneous
policy enforcement

00:18:25.630 --> 00:18:30.370
and monitoring capability,
while behind the proxy,

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:33.040
you can have different
languages, different deployment

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:36.520
models, and different RPC
protocols, for example.

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:45.490
So how does Istio enable
you to secure every service?

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:48.860
Istio does three simple
concepts, but three

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:50.990
very powerful capabilities.

00:18:50.990 --> 00:18:55.140
It enables you to do mTLS for
authentication encryption,

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:57.170
and it allows you to do
this without any changes

00:18:57.170 --> 00:18:59.210
to your application code.

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:01.580
Second, it allows you
to authorize access

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:05.280
based on service identity
or any channel attribute.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:11.000
So thus, Istio enable
you to create policies

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:13.520
that allow you to define
who can talk to whom

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:16.220
and then monitor those
policies centrally.

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:20.180
Lastly, if you are using
modern RPC protocols,

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:24.470
like REST or gRPC, then you
can configure finer=grained

00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:27.680
RPC-level access controls, and
we'll talk a bit about what

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:28.400
those are.

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:30.740
And these access
controls are what

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.260
really provides the
rich capabilities

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:35.910
that we leverage within
Google's production environment,

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:36.410
for example.

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:43.490
So why do we support
mTLS in Istio?

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:46.220
Well, it's policy driven
encryption in transit,

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:49.220
and as I said earlier, with
no application changes.

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:51.050
So that is a pretty
powerful paradigm,

00:19:51.050 --> 00:19:56.750
because it's very complicated
to write good TLS code

00:19:56.750 --> 00:19:59.180
and secure your keys and
maintain your certificates.

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:00.480
That's a lot of work.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:05.960
So that's great value, but
it's the obvious value.

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:08.030
One of the key
principles that we

00:20:08.030 --> 00:20:10.100
have within Google's
production environment,

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:13.270
which actually is
enabled through mTLS,

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:14.930
is strong authentication.

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:16.844
This is something
that's not so obvious

00:20:16.844 --> 00:20:19.010
but is a very critical piece
of the security posture

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:21.950
that we enable through Istio.

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:26.240
So the authentication model
that we enable via Istio

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:29.450
is that the peers are
always authenticated

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:31.860
using non-replayable
service identities.

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:35.000
And we make those identities
non-replayable by binding them

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:36.230
through the TLS channel.

00:20:36.230 --> 00:20:38.500
So you might call them
channel-bound service

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:39.380
identities.

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:43.190
Thus those identities cannot
be replayed by the recipient

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:48.680
or anybody who is trying to
sniff on the wire, because A,

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.230
they're encrypted on the
wire through TLS, and B,

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:54.380
the recipient where you actually
terminate the TLS does not have

00:20:54.380 --> 00:21:02.364
the private keys in order to be
able to replay the identities.

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:07.920
A very critical piece
here to understand

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.420
is that this does
not necessarily

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:12.090
represent your application
level identity.

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:13.830
So to take a very
concrete example,

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:16.470
if your service B were
to be a MySQL database,

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.540
you would still have
a MySQL level identity

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:21.420
that you propagate, which
the MySQL database is using

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:24.200
for fine-grain authorization,
like which table,

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:28.560
which row, et cetera,
do you have access to.

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:32.640
That floats on top as part
of your call to MySQL.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:35.910
What we are enforcing here is
the notion of a service such

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:39.000
that even if you had the
valid MySQL credentials,

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:41.550
we want to ensure that you
are actually a service that

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:44.270
is authorized to talk to this
specific MySQL database--

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:47.640
so service-to-service trust
versus application-level trust.

00:21:53.090 --> 00:21:57.350
So one of the things we realized
after we launched this was that

00:21:57.350 --> 00:22:02.550
customers cannot go from no
mTLS to all mTLS on day one.

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:04.760
The reason is you mostly
will have services

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:06.560
that have many clients.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:09.030
Sometimes you don't even realize
the clients that you have

00:22:09.030 --> 00:22:11.780
for a given service, and
you cannot imagine that all

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:15.200
the clients and their clients
and their clients all get

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:18.050
enabled with mTLS on day one.

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:22.890
So because of that we created
this mode within Istio

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:24.950
called permissive
mode, which allows

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:29.610
you to incrementally apply
mTLS to an existing service.

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:31.940
So for example, you can
start with a service that

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:34.040
pertains to your
most sensitive data

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:37.520
and then take the most
sensitive client part, a path

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:40.070
that you want to
secure, and just secure

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:43.610
that first while still allowing
requests from other clients.

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:45.420
For example, a health
checker service

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:47.030
may be also accessing
that service,

00:22:47.030 --> 00:22:50.131
but you don't need that to
be mTLS-enabled on day one.

00:22:50.131 --> 00:22:52.630
But your health check service
doesn't break just because you

00:22:52.630 --> 00:22:54.890
have secured your data path.

00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:57.890
And this is a very
powerful capability

00:22:57.890 --> 00:22:59.600
that we have built
into Istio and would

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:01.220
be available as
part of version 1.0,

00:23:01.220 --> 00:23:05.920
which we are launching shortly

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:09.340
Once we have a strongly
authenticated service identity,

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:13.097
we can now use that
for authorization.

00:23:13.097 --> 00:23:14.680
And to understand
Istio authorization,

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:17.330
you have to understand
the notion of a service.

00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:20.760
A service has a bit
of a dual identity.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:22.840
It's both a network
identity as well as

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:24.430
an application identity.

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:28.210
And here, identity, I don't mean
in terms of actual credentials,

00:23:28.210 --> 00:23:31.000
but in terms of personas.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:33.010
It can be thought of
as a network entity,

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:35.770
or it can be thought of as
an application level entity.

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:39.510
So a service, therefore,
spans the stack

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.820
that I've shown
here, which is you

00:23:42.820 --> 00:23:46.120
can have a service be
understanding channel level

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:47.710
attributes, like IP address.

00:23:47.710 --> 00:23:49.300
The service identity
that's on the TLS

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:52.060
channel or the Kubernetes
namespace, for example,

00:23:52.060 --> 00:23:55.660
as part of the context
in which it is called.

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:58.270
At the application level, the
first thing it can understand

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:01.420
is your API or RPC-level
artifacts for protocols

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:03.190
like HTTP or gRBC.

00:24:03.190 --> 00:24:06.070
So for example, what's the
URI, what operation is getting

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:08.890
called on it, and what
are the various claims

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:11.980
that are encoded in a
JWT token, for example.

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:16.960
So thus, you can use a service
level authorization policy

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:19.600
like Istio's policy
to do both what

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:21.220
traditional
micro-segmentation allowed

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:23.620
you to do by leveraging
channel attributes

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:26.500
and saying who can talk to whom,
just in pure tones of channel

00:24:26.500 --> 00:24:31.840
attributes like namespaces, for
example, or just identities.

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:33.910
A key thing which
Istio enables you to do

00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:35.220
is to trust that identity.

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.720
So you can actually do something
that's more natural to you.

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:40.695
As in, which service can talk
to which, versus which namespace

00:24:40.695 --> 00:24:41.770
can talk to which.

00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:44.740
Which is little second order in
direction of what you're really

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:46.276
trying to express.

00:24:46.276 --> 00:24:47.650
This is more closer
to the intent

00:24:47.650 --> 00:24:49.810
that you want to express
by using identities.

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:52.760
But a very powerful
thing that is

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:54.790
enabled though
Istio is to combine

00:24:54.790 --> 00:24:56.470
this notion of a
service identity

00:24:56.470 --> 00:24:59.190
with elements that
are available to you

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:02.440
at the API or the RPC level.

00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:06.790
And then create a
powerful mechanism

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:09.310
to determine a
composite policy, like,

00:25:09.310 --> 00:25:11.380
for example, what
operations can be performed

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:14.440
by whom and through which path.

00:25:14.440 --> 00:25:17.350
Let's take a concrete example
to understand such a policy.

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:23.380
So here's an example.

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:28.300
Joe here is an authorized user
and is a support engineer,

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:32.260
for example, who
has access to PII.

00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:35.830
But the only way he
should have access to PII,

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:37.540
which is the
organization's policy,

00:25:37.540 --> 00:25:39.430
is by using a CASE tool.

00:25:39.430 --> 00:25:41.177
And the CASE tool then
validates that Joe

00:25:41.177 --> 00:25:44.830
is making an access with
the proper case identity,

00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:45.410
for example.

00:25:45.410 --> 00:25:47.350
There's really a
case, a valid case.

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:50.890
And the user's data
that Joe is fetching

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:51.982
is relevant to that case.

00:25:51.982 --> 00:25:53.440
And there's a
business logic that's

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:54.820
built into the CASE tool.

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:59.140
The back end is configured
to say Joe has access to user

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:04.300
data, however, Joe cannot
directly access the back end.

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:08.500
In such a scenario,
Joe then decides

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:10.720
it's pretty inconvenient
for me to do this CASE tool.

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:13.180
I need to do some research
on a particular issue

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:15.530
and look at it across
a bunch of users.

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:17.350
Why don't I just
create my own little BI

00:26:17.350 --> 00:26:21.700
tool, a dinky little BI tool,
and then enable it with mTLS.

00:26:21.700 --> 00:26:24.520
I obviously have access
to PII on the back end,

00:26:24.520 --> 00:26:26.890
and just use the tool
to then access the PII.

00:26:26.890 --> 00:26:30.040
Now this, while from a
back end perspective,

00:26:30.040 --> 00:26:32.290
application-level perspective,
seems just fine, right?

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:35.730
Joe is authorized at
application level to access PII.

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:39.130
But now you're
bypassing a tool that

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:42.640
was meant to implement a bunch
of the organization's policies.

00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:45.040
And that should not be allowed.

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:47.650
So here's an example
of a policy file

00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:51.370
that not only checks that
the user on the chart

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:53.512
belongs to a particular
role, like reader--

00:26:53.512 --> 00:26:55.720
so for example, you could
substitute that by support,

00:26:55.720 --> 00:26:57.497
for example, that has
been authenticated,

00:26:57.497 --> 00:26:59.080
say, from your Active
Directory system

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:01.810
and then encoded as a
JWT claim, but it also

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:06.790
checks that the service
level identity is actually

00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:11.920
the CASE tool and not the dinky
little app that Joe created.

00:27:11.920 --> 00:27:14.170
Now, when you implement
such a policy,

00:27:14.170 --> 00:27:16.030
it now ensures that
you have locked down

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:18.730
the path for privileged
access to ensure

00:27:18.730 --> 00:27:20.650
that only an authorized
user can access it,

00:27:20.650 --> 00:27:23.870
but very importantly, using
just the right client.

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:25.720
This is a very
powerful principle

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:28.150
that is enabled to protect
highly sensitive PII

00:27:28.150 --> 00:27:33.960
data or other kinds of sensitive
data from insider access.

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:36.450
And this was
something, for example,

00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:39.540
it's just not possible through
traditional micro-segmentation

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:40.200
approach.

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:42.950
Micro-segmentation still
enables you to prevent

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:44.255
lateral movement of threats.

00:27:44.255 --> 00:27:45.630
There are other
benefits to doing

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:49.080
classic micro-segmentation, but
here you get benefits of both.

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:51.360
Through mutual
TLS, we explain how

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:53.852
you can prevent lateral
movement of threats

00:27:53.852 --> 00:27:55.560
through the implementation
of mutual TLS,

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:58.080
and now with these kind
of authorization policies,

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:00.090
we show how you can
protect sensitive data

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:02.275
from unauthorized
insider access.

00:28:06.220 --> 00:28:09.650
Lastly, as I mentioned, you
need to monitor your posture.

00:28:09.650 --> 00:28:12.540
Once you have
implementing policies

00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:15.490
at scale like this over a
large number of services,

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:18.660
you do need to have a consistent
visibility of security posture.

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:21.220
And that's what Istio really
enables you to do, is--

00:28:23.836 --> 00:28:25.210
everything that
I described could

00:28:25.210 --> 00:28:28.180
have been implemented within
your application logic.

00:28:28.180 --> 00:28:29.744
The reason to do it
in Istio is, one,

00:28:29.744 --> 00:28:30.910
you free up your developers.

00:28:30.910 --> 00:28:33.940
Your developers can just
focus on business logic,

00:28:33.940 --> 00:28:36.937
and not everyone is
a security expert.

00:28:36.937 --> 00:28:38.770
So they can move very
quickly without having

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:41.200
to learn all of these concepts.

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:43.270
But more importantly,
the developer

00:28:43.270 --> 00:28:45.050
can use a language
of their choice.

00:28:45.050 --> 00:28:47.000
They can use an RPC
protocol of their choice.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:50.740
They can use a deployment
paradigm of their choice.

00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:52.600
And you can move things around.

00:28:52.600 --> 00:28:55.800
You can go from VM to containers
to cloud or back on prem.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:59.950
And while doing so, as a
security administration

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:02.230
function, you'll still have
a consistent visibility

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:03.896
of your security
posture, no matter what

00:29:03.896 --> 00:29:05.860
is happening on the stack.

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:09.860
This allows you to go through
a whole IT transformation

00:29:09.860 --> 00:29:11.580
while maintaining
a security posture

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:12.990
and you're defensing
the posture.

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:14.860
So this is another
very powerful paradigm

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:21.260
that Istio enables
you to practice.

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:24.710
With that, it's time to take
a look at how this actually

00:29:24.710 --> 00:29:28.670
works in practice with
Istio as it stands today.

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:32.380
For that I'll invite Tao to
walk you through a quick demo.

00:29:32.380 --> 00:29:34.528
Thank you.

00:29:34.528 --> 00:29:39.606
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:39.606 --> 00:29:40.730
TAO LI: So hello, everyone.

00:29:40.730 --> 00:29:42.444
My name is Tao Li.

00:29:42.444 --> 00:29:44.860
Today I am going to give you
a demo about Istio's security

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:45.360
features.

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:51.890
So the agenda includes--

00:29:51.890 --> 00:29:55.450
[MICROPHONE FEEDBACK]

00:29:55.450 --> 00:29:57.950
SAMRAT RAY: Do you want
to use mine instead?

00:29:57.950 --> 00:30:00.530
TAO LI: Can I have just a mic?

00:30:00.530 --> 00:30:01.361
Oh, OK.

00:30:01.361 --> 00:30:01.860
OK.

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:03.810
So the agenda
includes four items.

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:06.090
The first one is the
policy-based mTLS

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:07.200
authentication.

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:10.760
The second one is
identity-based authorization.

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:14.160
The third one is about end
user identity verification,

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:17.430
and the fourth
one is monitoring.

00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:20.880
And I'm going to use a
pretty simple example

00:30:20.880 --> 00:30:24.930
to walk you through what
Istio security can do for you.

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:28.380
Let's say you are
developing this simple app.

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:31.290
This one has a front
end and have a back end.

00:30:31.290 --> 00:30:33.960
So the end user call
the front end and front

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:35.570
end will get the
data from back end

00:30:35.570 --> 00:30:37.630
and send it back to end user.

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:40.810
So this is pretty simple,
but in this model,

00:30:40.810 --> 00:30:43.500
you can see currently
there is no traffic

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:47.520
encryption and no access
control and no monitoring.

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:52.410
So what we can do is
once you install Istio,

00:30:52.410 --> 00:30:55.180
Istio can handle all the
security stuff for you.

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:57.420
So you don't have to
spend a lot of effort

00:30:57.420 --> 00:31:01.080
to implement security
code, and Istio will

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:03.240
handle this feature for you.

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:06.720
You can focus on your
own business logic.

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:09.870
So as you can see here,
once we deploy Istio,

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:13.860
Istio is a sidecar proxy
that deployed right next

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:14.970
to your service.

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:17.760
Each of your servers, you
have an Istio sidecar,

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:20.550
and this proxy
will intercept all

00:31:20.550 --> 00:31:23.310
the incoming and
outgoing traffic.

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:26.190
And besides that, Istio
have a ingress path.

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:29.220
So this one is also
an envoy-based proxy,

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:32.340
and all incoming traffic
will go through ingress

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:34.890
and then go to our front end.

00:31:34.890 --> 00:31:37.470
So this model has a problem.

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:40.510
Let's say somebody
hacked into your network,

00:31:40.510 --> 00:31:42.300
was in your own network.

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:45.600
So within your
network, the hacker

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:49.140
can talk to the back end, even
when your back end has some PII

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:50.410
information.

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:53.430
So how do we solve that?

00:31:53.430 --> 00:31:56.660
So with Istio,
you can force mTLS

00:31:56.660 --> 00:32:00.680
once you enforce mutual TLS on
your front end and back end.

00:32:03.580 --> 00:32:08.010
So in that way, the client needs
to provide a valid certificate.

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:10.250
So since this
hacker was in there,

00:32:10.250 --> 00:32:12.840
they only hack into the
network, but you're not

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:15.600
having enough privilege
to join the mass, which

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:18.270
means you don't have a
proxy that will show you

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:21.390
that this attack
will be stopped.

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:25.260
And this about what
mutual TLS can do.

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:28.410
Of course, mutual TLS can
encrypt the traffic for you.

00:32:28.410 --> 00:32:33.220
So sometimes we feel like the
mutual TLS are not enough.

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:35.130
We need some access control.

00:32:35.130 --> 00:32:38.010
Imagine you have a front
end and a back end,

00:32:38.010 --> 00:32:42.330
but normally you can have
like 100 or 1,000 other apps.

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:44.470
For example, in
Samrat's example,

00:32:44.470 --> 00:32:46.950
you might have a logging app.

00:32:46.950 --> 00:32:49.830
So if this one get
compromised, this logging

00:32:49.830 --> 00:32:52.380
belongs to a totally
different department

00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:54.570
and is not supposed
to call the back end.

00:32:54.570 --> 00:32:56.700
But because this
one has a proxy,

00:32:56.700 --> 00:32:59.670
this one could get a key cert
from our Citadel, our control

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:00.430
plane.

00:33:00.430 --> 00:33:05.280
So chances are, this logging,
once it gets compromise,

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:08.250
so bad guy can hack
into the logging system

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:12.060
and call the back end to get
your sensitive information,

00:33:12.060 --> 00:33:13.900
like your credit
card information.

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:19.590
So what we can do is we just
deploy Istio access control

00:33:19.590 --> 00:33:20.970
on our back end.

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:23.380
So once we have
this one deployed,

00:33:23.380 --> 00:33:27.060
we will specify that only front
end can access the back end.

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:30.630
So that the logging, even
if you had a valid key cert,

00:33:30.630 --> 00:33:33.450
you cannot talk to the back end.

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:37.530
So we're talking about
two inside attackers,

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:40.440
and we haven't really
focused on the end users.

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:43.260
So right now, from
end user, end user

00:33:43.260 --> 00:33:45.570
has not provided any token yet.

00:33:45.570 --> 00:33:50.010
So what if we have a malicious
guy outside of the network,

00:33:50.010 --> 00:33:51.780
and they're trying
to do the same thing

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:53.550
as our normal guy do?

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:55.740
So they will try to
call our front end,

00:33:55.740 --> 00:33:57.810
so the traffic will
still go through.

00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:02.670
So chances are you can also
implement your own end user

00:34:02.670 --> 00:34:05.320
validation system, but
Istio provides this for you.

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:07.320
So you don't have to
really worry about it.

00:34:07.320 --> 00:34:08.790
And what we can do--

00:34:08.790 --> 00:34:12.060
I'm going to show you is when
we enforce this end user token

00:34:12.060 --> 00:34:18.239
check at our front end so
the hackers cannot talk

00:34:18.239 --> 00:34:21.420
to the front end any more
because they don't have a valid

00:34:21.420 --> 00:34:22.050
token.

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:26.294
But for our normal person,
for example, our admin,

00:34:26.294 --> 00:34:27.710
you can actually
have the access--

00:34:27.710 --> 00:34:30.150
you have access to
the end user token,

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:31.679
for example, the JWT token.

00:34:31.679 --> 00:34:35.707
So you are able to
access the network.

00:34:35.707 --> 00:34:37.290
And the last one I'm
going to show you

00:34:37.290 --> 00:34:39.150
about the monitoring stuff.

00:34:39.150 --> 00:34:40.529
So let's look at the demo.

00:34:40.529 --> 00:34:41.820
Can you switch to demo, please?

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:48.730
OK, so lets look at it.

00:34:48.730 --> 00:34:51.750
I already have this demo set up.

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:53.370
So you can look here--

00:34:53.370 --> 00:34:56.159
I have a product page
which is our front end,

00:34:56.159 --> 00:34:58.530
and we have details,
which are the back end.

00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:01.020
Each of them already
have a sidecar,

00:35:01.020 --> 00:35:03.120
because it has the
two contenders.

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:06.990
And I have Istio control
plane deployed already.

00:35:06.990 --> 00:35:10.900
So right now there is no Istio
security feature applied yet.

00:35:10.900 --> 00:35:15.360
So if we look at this page,
this our online web page.

00:35:15.360 --> 00:35:19.230
From external, you can
access this web page.

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:22.200
But you can see here, we have
some sensitive information

00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:24.100
like credit card information.

00:35:24.100 --> 00:35:26.820
And as I mentioned, the first
thing I'm going to show you

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:30.540
is if somebody hacks
into your network,

00:35:30.540 --> 00:35:34.290
for example, this is a GKE node.

00:35:34.290 --> 00:35:36.220
This one is within the network.

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:37.845
So if someone hack
into the network--

00:35:40.800 --> 00:35:44.810
so what this guy can do,
it will call our back end.

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:46.760
This is the IP address
of our back end.

00:35:46.760 --> 00:35:50.370
So they can call back and to
gather sensitive information.

00:35:50.370 --> 00:35:54.200
For example, you can see
the credit card shows here.

00:35:54.200 --> 00:36:02.020
So what we can do is
we apply mTLS policy.

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:05.000
By the way, this K is
just my alias for kubectl.

00:36:08.110 --> 00:36:10.390
So right now the
policy is deployed.

00:36:10.390 --> 00:36:12.460
Let's look at the policy first.

00:36:12.460 --> 00:36:18.110
So this policy defines enforced
mTLS on default namespace.

00:36:18.110 --> 00:36:20.440
So our front end and
back end both deployed

00:36:20.440 --> 00:36:21.890
on default namespace.

00:36:21.890 --> 00:36:23.660
So once you deploy
this page, you

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:27.520
will see that the front end and
back end both required a peer

00:36:27.520 --> 00:36:29.980
to present a valid cert.

00:36:29.980 --> 00:36:35.110
So that means, for this case,
if you're within the network

00:36:35.110 --> 00:36:38.700
but you don't present a
key cert, you will see--

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:40.390
you don't present
a client cert, you

00:36:40.390 --> 00:36:42.910
see the traffic got rejected.

00:36:42.910 --> 00:36:46.410
And from our normal
web page, it always

00:36:46.410 --> 00:36:49.770
work because it's from ingress,
and to front and back end,

00:36:49.770 --> 00:36:52.290
everyone has proxy.

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:54.270
So this is mTLS.

00:36:54.270 --> 00:36:55.770
The second thing
I want to show you

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:58.800
is about the access control.

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:01.110
I mentioned, we have
a log-in system,

00:37:01.110 --> 00:37:10.050
and I actually called it
asleep path in my case.

00:37:10.050 --> 00:37:11.700
So we have a path here.

00:37:11.700 --> 00:37:17.030
This one had a sidecar
deployed, and I'm

00:37:17.030 --> 00:37:21.470
going to show you that
when I SSH into this part,

00:37:21.470 --> 00:37:24.220
I can talk to the back end.

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:27.820
So you can see this
is the credit card

00:37:27.820 --> 00:37:29.890
information, shows up here.

00:37:29.890 --> 00:37:33.810
Either you have mTLS, because
this guy also have a client

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:36.430
certificate, so he
cannot prevent this.

00:37:36.430 --> 00:37:46.160
And what I can do is I will
apply this access policy.

00:37:46.160 --> 00:37:48.590
Now let's take a
look at the policy.

00:37:48.590 --> 00:37:55.390
So what this policy does, it
enabled RBAC on the default

00:37:55.390 --> 00:37:58.210
namespace right here--

00:37:58.210 --> 00:38:01.460
by the way, RBAC is a
Role-Based Access Control.

00:38:01.460 --> 00:38:04.420
So Istio uses the RBAC
to do the access control,

00:38:04.420 --> 00:38:09.100
and this one defines RBAC on
our default namespace, which

00:38:09.100 --> 00:38:11.170
is our front end and back end.

00:38:11.170 --> 00:38:13.930
So let's look at the
actual service row.

00:38:13.930 --> 00:38:16.960
We have two rows.

00:38:16.960 --> 00:38:21.580
The first service row is
about the product page,

00:38:21.580 --> 00:38:22.930
which is our front end.

00:38:22.930 --> 00:38:26.120
So this one defines a get
permission to the front end.

00:38:26.120 --> 00:38:29.920
And you can see the user's a
star, which means right now,

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:32.860
in this policy, everyone can
have access to the front end.

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:35.920
Of course, you can set
more strict policy.

00:38:35.920 --> 00:38:38.810
But the more interesting
thing is here.

00:38:38.810 --> 00:38:42.170
So if you look at here,
another service row

00:38:42.170 --> 00:38:46.030
defines the get permission to
the details, to the back end.

00:38:46.030 --> 00:38:50.020
And who do we bind
this permission to?

00:38:50.020 --> 00:38:52.030
You can look at
here, this is a user.

00:38:52.030 --> 00:38:54.500
This product page
is our front end.

00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:56.740
So once we deploy
this policy, you

00:38:56.740 --> 00:39:00.050
can see that only front
end can access back end.

00:39:00.050 --> 00:39:04.990
But everything now, if you
have a client certificate,

00:39:04.990 --> 00:39:07.190
you cannot access anymore.

00:39:07.190 --> 00:39:10.590
So let's take a look.

00:39:10.590 --> 00:39:13.380
So this is the one
I tried before.

00:39:13.380 --> 00:39:15.740
So once we take a
look at this one,

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:18.740
you will see there should
be some error here--

00:39:18.740 --> 00:39:21.050
403 forbidden.

00:39:21.050 --> 00:39:23.270
So once we have
this, you can see,

00:39:23.270 --> 00:39:25.610
if you have clients certificate,
you cannot access it

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:29.300
because you don't have the--
we set the access control.

00:39:29.300 --> 00:39:33.860
And from here, since I set up
the access control properly,

00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:35.370
so the front end can access it.

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:37.880
So this page is still there.

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:41.900
So this is about mTLS
and the access control.

00:39:41.900 --> 00:39:45.720
So this third one I want to
show you is about the end user.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:49.820
So the end user is,
sometimes, as I mentioned,

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:53.390
we want to define
some policies such

00:39:53.390 --> 00:39:58.150
that the end user needs to
present me some valid token.

00:39:58.150 --> 00:40:00.290
Otherwise I'm going to stop you.

00:40:00.290 --> 00:40:03.710
So if you look at this example,
right now from this web

00:40:03.710 --> 00:40:05.930
page, the end user--

00:40:05.930 --> 00:40:08.660
the browser does not
provide any token yet.

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:10.760
But this traffic can
still go through.

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:13.160
So I'm going to
enforce some policy.

00:40:13.160 --> 00:40:14.360
Let's take a look.

00:40:18.040 --> 00:40:21.540
So I deploy this JWT
authentication policy.

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:24.450
Let's take a look at
this policy first.

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:27.600
So this policy applies to
our product page, which

00:40:27.600 --> 00:40:30.900
is our front end, and
it requires the peer

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:34.920
to present a certificate,
which the peer is mutual TLS,

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:37.620
and a region besides the peer.

00:40:37.620 --> 00:40:40.830
We also want to specify,
say a region of this color

00:40:40.830 --> 00:40:42.160
will need to--

00:40:42.160 --> 00:40:43.490
we require a JWT.

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:46.890
So this is a issuer
of the JWT, and this

00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:50.220
is where you can
fetch the public key

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:52.800
to validate this JWT.

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:56.000
So once we specify this,
let's look at this page.

00:40:58.730 --> 00:41:00.980
The policy may take
a while to propagate.

00:41:03.750 --> 00:41:06.270
So you can see this
original authentication

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:10.200
failed, because this one
doesn't have a valid token.

00:41:10.200 --> 00:41:13.380
And what I can do is I prepared
a valid token, actually,

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:15.120
right here, beforehand.

00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:16.830
And let's see.

00:41:16.830 --> 00:41:20.430
From this page, this one
still works because this guy

00:41:20.430 --> 00:41:22.470
present a valid token.

00:41:22.470 --> 00:41:26.050
And this is about end
user verification.

00:41:26.050 --> 00:41:28.110
The last one I want to
show you in the demo

00:41:28.110 --> 00:41:29.820
is about the monitoring.

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:33.330
So Istio Integrate
was a Grafana,

00:41:33.330 --> 00:41:39.210
which is an open source tool
for you to monitor the traffic.

00:41:39.210 --> 00:41:42.810
So from here, as
you can see here,

00:41:42.810 --> 00:41:44.040
we have a different service.

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:45.750
You can select a
different service

00:41:45.750 --> 00:41:47.840
and you can select
different stuff.

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:53.640
And the x-axis is the time,
and the y-axis is the QPS.

00:41:53.640 --> 00:41:55.400
So you can see here.

00:41:55.400 --> 00:42:01.810
This red line means the default
production page default 200.

00:42:01.810 --> 00:42:04.830
So this one is before
we enforced mTLS.

00:42:04.830 --> 00:42:06.690
So you can see this
is the first traffic.

00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:10.110
So after we enforced
mTLS, you can see this--

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:13.080
the product page
default 200 mTLS.

00:42:13.080 --> 00:42:15.060
So from here, all
the green lines

00:42:15.060 --> 00:42:18.360
means something that is
the traffic generated

00:42:18.360 --> 00:42:21.450
after we deployed mTLS.

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:24.240
And besides, we also
have some purple lines.

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:27.390
The purple lines
means we had 401s.

00:42:27.390 --> 00:42:32.340
That's because we called this
one, the traffic got rejected.

00:42:32.340 --> 00:42:35.850
So that's pretty much
about the demo part.

00:42:35.850 --> 00:42:37.500
We talked about mTLS.

00:42:37.500 --> 00:42:39.300
We talked about access control.

00:42:39.300 --> 00:42:41.490
We talked about end
user verification.

00:42:41.490 --> 00:42:43.830
We talked about the monitoring.

00:42:43.830 --> 00:42:48.240
And the last thing, this is
pretty much about the demo.

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:52.030
And switch back to
the slides, please.

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:52.530
OK.

00:42:55.890 --> 00:42:59.490
So beside that, we'll
have more stuff coming.

00:42:59.490 --> 00:43:04.430
As you can see here, we have
Istio 1.0 coming pretty quick,

00:43:04.430 --> 00:43:08.400
